n  in the neighborhood are working on and  you know they it promises to like make  them a whole bunch of money now  you know and i know that james is a  vampire  but obviously the characters in the  novel wouldn't jump to that conclusion  immediately as you wouldn't in real life  patricia really only begins to suspect  that something is maybe not entirely  kosher about their charming neighbor  when she hears through their cleaning  lady mrs green  that  little kids and teenagers in the black  part of town which is called six mile  have been disappearing uh turning up  dead or sometimes committing suicide  after some unexplained illness  and at each of these times uh people in  the community who are very distrustful  of strangers because of this stuff  that's been going on  have seen a white van now mrs green  actually wrote down a partial license  plate for this white van which she saw  at one time and it so happens that this  white van has the same partial license  plate as james harris's white van so as  the danger escalates patricia sees  some very messed up stuff with their own  eyes which again i don't want to spoil  but  the tension in the book and a lot of the  scares and terror in the book  it doesn't come from it's not like  suspense over trying to figure out who  the vampire is or like what or trying to  identify him because you know who the  vampire is like pretty much from the  second you meet the guy i mean you know  there's gonna be a vampire and this is a  stranger and all this weird [ __ ] starts  happening so obviously he's the vampire  but the suspense and the scary [ __ ]  comes from the fact that patricia and  even more so as it goes on  knows absolutely that james is a monster  but absolutely no one except for mrs  green  believes her even when patricia tries to  do the smart thing i mean obviously she  doesn't come right out and say you know  he's a vampire because everybody would  think it would be like some fright night  situation i just thin